-- title:
--   The solvable radical $\mathrm{srad}(G)$
-- statement:
--   For a group $G$, the **solvable radical** $\mathrm{srad}(G)$ is the subgroup generated by all solvable normal subgroups of $G$. It is normal.
--
--   For a finite group the product of two solvable normal subgroups is again solvable and normal, so $\mathrm{srad}(G)$ is the **largest solvable normal subgroup** of $G$ — the paper's definition (§3.5 (vii), p.20). The paper's group isomorphism algorithm recurses along a characteristic series through $\mathrm{srad}(G)$ and treats the quotient $G/\mathrm{srad}(G)$, which has trivial solvable radical, separately.
-- source:
--   Qiao, Zhang, Testing isomorphism of finite groups in time polynomial in the group order, preprint, Nov. 3, 2026 (held version sha256 eb9a3355…), §3.5 (vii), p.20 (PDF p.24); §9.2.1, p.94 (PDF p.98)

import Mathlib

set_option autoImplicit false

namespace GroupIsoPoly

/-- **The solvable radical** `srad(G)`.

Qiao, Zhang, *Testing isomorphism of finite groups in time polynomial in the group order*, preprint,
Nov. 3, 2026 (held version sha256 eb9a3355…), §3.5 (vii), p.20 (PDF p.24): "Solvable radical:
compute srad(Q), the largest solvable normal subgroup of Q."

`srad G` is the subgroup generated by all solvable normal subgroups of `G`.

**Formalization Note.** For a finite group the product of two solvable normal subgroups is solvable
and normal, so this supremum is itself solvable and is the largest solvable normal subgroup. -/
def srad (G : Type*) [Group G] : Subgroup G :=
  sSup {N : Subgroup G | N.Normal ∧ Group.IsSolvable N}

instance srad_normal (G : Type*) [Group G] : (srad G).Normal :=
  Subgroup.sSup_normal _ fun _ h => h.1

end GroupIsoPoly


